window.BIZ_PAPERS = (window.BIZ_PAPERS || []).concat([{"id": "CONS2-SAM", "unit": 2, "key": "SAM", "series": "Sample assessment (AAQ)", "date": "Pearson sample assessment material", "title": "Pearson sample paper", "source": "pearson", "kind": "exam", "code": "Sample", "spec": "new", "total": 70, "minutes": 105, "items": [{"q": 1, "sub": "", "marks": 1, "section": "A", "ctx": [], "text": "Structural steelwork needs to be protected from fire in order to retain its structural\nintegrity\nIdentify one fire protection method used for structural steelwork.\nA Timber cladding\nB Multi-layer plasterboard\nC Polystyrene insulation\nD Aggregate", "ms": {"body": "B – Multi-layer plasterboard", "levels": null}}, {"q": 2, "sub": "", "marks": 2, "section": "A", "ctx": [], "text": "State two ways that replacement piles transfer building loads to the bearing strata in\norder to support a building.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for each correct stated method\nof support up to a maximum of two marks.\n• End bearing/transfer of loads to deeper higher\nload bearing strata/acting like a column\nthrough the soil (1)\n• Friction/using the resistance between the pile\nand the soil along its length (1)", "levels": null}}, {"q": 3, "sub": "", "marks": 4, "section": "A", "ctx": [], "text": "Explain two health risks to the construction worker associated with using in-situ\nconcrete.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for a correct health risk and\none additional mark for appropriate justification\nof that risk up to a maximum of four marks.\n• Cement burns/dermatitis from skin contact (1)\nbecause cement has a very high alkali content\n• Casting concrete in situ/inhalation of\nconcrete/cement dust could cause respiratory\nproblems (1) because concrete dust carries a\nhigh volume of silica’ (1)\n• Casting concrete in situ could cause eye\nirritation/temporary blindness/permeant\nblindness (1) because the chemicals in\nconcrete dust react with the [water/moisture]\nin the eye (1)\nDo not award marks for any response that\nrelates to safety as the response must be related\nto health issues.\nMarking points may be reversed/given in any\norder.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 4, "sub": "a", "marks": 4, "section": "A", "ctx": ["A local company is constructing a two-storey hotel building comprising the following: • 60 twin bedrooms (30 per floor) accessed via a central corridor • Emergency staircase to either end of the corridor, with external fire exit door • Modular construction to the bedroom areas and structural steel frame to the public areas • Reception area and office • Bar • Breakfast area with associated kitchen • Air conditioning to the public areas • Ceiling height in public areas 3.2m • Ceiling height in bedroom areas 2.4m"], "text": "The bar, reception area and breakfast room will have suspended ceilings.\nExplain two potential benefits of installing suspended ceilings in these areas.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for identification and one\nadditional mark for appropriate\nexpansion/justification up to a maximum of four\nmarks.\n• The public areas can have higher ceilings (1)\nbecause the suspension wires allow the ceiling\nto be placed at the correct/most appropriate\nlevel (1)\n• The void above the suspended ceiling will\naccommodate ductwork and ventilation\nequipment (1) because the public areas will\nneed to have mechanical ventilation/air\nconditioning (1)\n• The suspended ceiling provides a single large\nflat soffit area (1) because structural\nelements/beams will need to be hidden (1)\n• Allows the designer to produce an\naesthetically pleasing interior in the public\nareas (1) because a variety of finishes are\navailable (1)\n• The suspended ceilings provide acoustic\nbenefits/can absorb sound (1) because they\nreduce reverberation/echo/providing a degree\nof privacy (1)\nMarking points may be reversed/given in any\norder.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 4, "sub": "b", "marks": 4, "section": "A", "ctx": [], "text": "The hotel will have a fire alarm system to facilitate early and safe evacuation of\nresidents in the event of a fire.\nExplain two features incorporated into the design of the hotel that would\nfacilitate safe evacuation in the event of a fire.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for identification and one\nadditional mark for appropriate\nexpansion/justification up to a maximum of four\nmarks.\n• Provision of two exit routes (1) because one\nmay be blocked by fire/ensuring that escape is\nalways possible (1)\n• Fire compartmentalisation (1) because this\nwill prevent the spread of fire to escape routes\n• Refuge areas (1) because this will allow a safe\nplace for elderly/less able people to await\nrescue (1)\n• Protected shafts (1) because will prevent\nspread of fire to the escape stairwells/exits (1)\n• Auto-closing fire doors with vision panels (1)\nbecause this will further compartmentalise the\nbuilding/allow people/firefighters to check the\nroute before opening the door (1)\nMarking points may be reversed/given in any\norder.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 5, "sub": "", "marks": 2, "section": "A", "ctx": [], "text": "An architect is considering using solid concrete ground floors for a new housing\ndevelopment.\nThe new development is being built on a steeply sloping site and ground that has\nrelatively poor bearing capacity until firm bearing is found at a depth of 1.5m.\nExplain one disadvantage of solid concrete ground floor construction for the houses\non this development.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for identification and one\nadditional mark for appropriate\nexpansion/justification up to a maximum of two\nmarks.\n• The use of retaining walls to mitigate against\nlandslip will be required (1) because cut and\nfill will be used to achieve a level surface (1)\n• Movement or settlement of the fill could occur\n(1) because in areas of deep fill it will be\ndifficult to achieve effective compaction (1)\n• Differential settlement between the walls and\nfloor may occur (1) because of poor ground\nsoil bearing capacity (1)\nMarking points may be reversed/given in any\norder.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 6, "sub": "", "marks": 9, "section": "A", "ctx": [], "text": "A hotel is considering installing photovoltaic panels to reduce its energy costs and\nCO2 emissions. The hotel is in a UK location that has a higher number of rainy days\nthan the UK average, but mean wind speeds are lower than the UK average.\nDiscuss the factors that the hotel would need to consider when deciding whether to\ninstall photovoltaic panels to contribute to the provision of energy for the project.", "ms": {"body": "Photovoltaic (PV) panels:\n• Photovoltaic panels are now established and\nreliable technology\n• Hotel rooms are occupied during the evening\nand overnight at a time the photovoltaic cells\nwill not be producing energy\n• Photovoltaic panels only produce energy\nduring daylight hours so will produce energy\nfor fewer hours in winter at the time demand\nfor electricity will be higher, but energy will be\navailable during the day when housekeeping\nactivities are underway\n• The location has a higher number of days of\nrainfall than the UK average and lower hours\nof sunshine which will have some impact on\nenergy production\n• The location has relatively low wind speeds\nwhich reduces the risk of uplift or damage to\nthe panels during inclement weather\nespecially as the panels are relatively fragile\nand easily damaged\n• Battery storage will be required to make most\nefficient use of the system because the peak\ndemand will be in the evenings/early\nmornings when guests are using the rooms or\nin the bar/dining room\n• Maintenance costs for PV panels are relatively\nlow because PV panels have no mechanical\nmoving parts that could fail or break down\n• PV panels are silent with no impact on guests\nstaying at the hotel\n• Solar power provides free and abundant\nenergy once the cost of the initial installation\nis covered but the high costs of installation\nmay impact on the level of specification that is\naffordable to stay within the hotel’s\nconstruction budget\n• Excess energy can be sold back to the grid via\nfeed-in tariffs to reduce the hotels costs or\noffered to the guests as free or reduced cost\ndaytime car charging.\n9 30\nLevel\nDescriptors\nThe second trait (application) carries twice as much weighting as trait 1\n(analysis).\n0\nNo rewardable material\n1\n1 - 3\n• Demonstrates a basic analysis of the\nsituation by superficially breaking down\nthe different aspects into their component\nparts.\n• Demonstrates basic application of\nknowledge and understanding that is\npartially relevant to the context of the\nquestion and may consider only one side of\nthe context.\n2\n4 - 6\n• Demonstrates a good analysis of the\nsituation by breaking down the different\naspects into their component parts.\n• Demonstrates good application of\nknowledge and understanding that is\nrelevant to the context of the question and\nconsiders both sides of the context.\n7 - 9\n• Demonstrates a thorough analysis of the\nsituation by comprehensively breaking\ndown the different aspects into their\ncomponent parts.\n• Demonstrates comprehensive application\nof knowledge and understanding that is\nconsistently relevant to the context of the\nquestion and considers both sides of the\ncontext in a balanced way.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 7, "sub": "", "marks": 9, "section": "A", "ctx": [], "text": "In the design of the hotel the architect is deciding whether to use modular\nconstruction methods with a mix of brick and timber exterior cladding.\nDiscuss the use of prefabricated modular construction, with a mix of timber and brick\nexterior cladding, for the hotel building.", "ms": {"body": "Modular:\n• A hotel is a good match and appropriate for\nmodular construction as each room (with\nensuite) can be fully fitted and finished in the\nfactory prior to delivery\n• Each room utilises four sided modules\n• Each room can have the external brick\ncladding and timber cladding applied in the\nfactory leaving only infill strips to be\ncompleted on site\n• Very fast construction method for this type of\n‘repetitive’ project as modules can be stacked\nside by side with only service connections\nneeding to be made\n• The hotel will be completed, commissioned\nand earning revenue quicker than other forms\nof construction\n• Open sided modules can be used for the\ncentral corridor\n• A mix of module types will be used for the\npublic areas some incorporating a linked\nstructural element\n• Separate stair and lift modules can be used\nfor the access to the upper floor\n• All electrical, heating installations and\ninsulation to each bedroom module are\ncompleted at the factory\n• Quality control checks in a factory\nenvironment will reduce the number of defects\npresent in the completed project\n• Factory production minimises the amount of\non-site waste\n• High standards of insulation are possible\nreducing the hotel’s heating bills/costs\n• Brick cladding provides a durable and\nwaterproof external barrier to the weather\n• Some types of timber cladding may need\nregular maintenance or treatment to retain\ndurability and prevent rot\n• Some timber such as Western red cedar and\nSiberian larch have natural resins/oils present,\nwhich reduces the need for maintenance\n9 32\nLevel\nDescriptors\nThe second trait (application) carries twice as much weighting as trait 1\n(analysis).\n0\nNo rewardable material\n1\n1 - 3\n• Demonstrates a basic analysis of the\nsituation by superficially breaking down\nthe different aspects into their component\nparts.\n• Demonstrates basic application of\nknowledge and understanding that is\npartially relevant to the context of the\nquestion and may consider only one side of\nthe context.\n2\n4 - 6\n• Demonstrates a good analysis of the\nsituation by breaking down the different\naspects into their component parts.\n• Demonstrates good application of\nknowledge and understanding that is\nrelevant to the context of the question and\nconsiders both sides of the context.\n7 - 9\n• Demonstrates a thorough analysis of the\nsituation by comprehensively breaking\ndown the different aspects into their\ncomponent parts.\n• Demonstrates comprehensive application\nof knowledge and understanding that is\nconsistently relevant to the context of the\nquestion and considers both sides of the\ncontext in a balanced way.\n33\nSection B", "levels": null}}, {"q": 8, "sub": "", "marks": 1, "section": "B", "ctx": [], "text": "State one type of structural frame that allows large clear spans with no intermediate\ncolumns for a single storey building.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for correct type of structural\nframe.\n• Portal frame (1)\n• Space frame (1)\n• Lattice girder frame (1)", "levels": null}}, {"q": 9, "sub": "", "marks": 2, "section": "B", "ctx": [], "text": "Construction projects involve excavation work which needs to be completed safely\nand efficiently.\nState two ways in which falls into open excavations can be prevented.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for each correct stated method\nof prevention up to a maximum of two marks.\n• Fences/barriers that directly prevent\naccidental falls into the excavation (1)\n• Signage that informs/warns the\nworkforce/public of the danger (1)\n• Ladder/safe access/egress when\nentering/leaving the excavation (1)", "levels": null}}, {"q": 10, "sub": "", "marks": 2, "section": "B", "ctx": [], "text": "A contractor aims to maintain an excellent health and safety record.\nExplain one way training might help to maintain high standards of health and safety.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for a benefit of training and\none additional mark for appropriate justification\nof that benefit up to a maximum of two marks.\n• Safe working practice and behaviour on site\nwill improve (1) because training will make\nworkers more aware of the hazards and risks\n(1)\n• Training will eliminate dangerous working\npractices (1) because workers are more aware\nof safe working practice (1)\n• Workers are more likely to comply with the\nuse of PPE (1) because training will make\nworkers aware of the benefits provided by PPE\n• Workers will be able to avoid working/moving\ninto in areas of increased site activity (1)\nbecause training will make them aware of\nspecific site issues/operations (1)\nMarking points may be reversed/given in any\norder.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 11, "sub": "", "marks": 2, "section": "B", "ctx": [], "text": "Traditional construction produces a lot of waste on site.\nExplain one benefit to the natural environment of the segregation of waste resulting\nfrom construction activity.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for a benefit of waste\nsegregation and one additional mark for\nappropriate justification of that benefit up to a\nmaximum of two marks.\n• Segregation of waste can reduce the leaching\nof pollutants/chemicals into the\nsoil/watercourses (1) because less waste is\nsent to landfill (1)\n• Segregation of waste can reduce the\nproduction of gases that contribute to climate\nchange (1) because less waste is sent to\nlandfill (1)\n• Segregation of waste reduces the depletion of\nscarce resources/reduces manufacturing\nemissions (1) because previously used\nmaterials are recycled (1)\nMarking points may be reversed/given in any\norder.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 12, "sub": "", "marks": 4, "section": "B", "ctx": [], "text": "Sketch and label a cross-section through a warm deck flat roof.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for each correctly\npositioned material/component up to\na maximum of four marks.\n• External roof membrane/roof felt/built up\nfelt/solar reflective finish/single ply\nmembrane/with optional additional plywood or\nmanufactured board deck (1)\n• Insulation/laser cut insulation/named\ninsulation type (1)\n• Roof decking with vapour\ncontrol/plywood/profiled steel\ndecking/manufactured board (1)\n• Structural support/steel/timber\njoist/rafter/sheeting rail/concrete deck/firring", "levels": null}}, {"q": 13, "sub": "", "marks": 2, "section": "B", "ctx": [], "text": "A survey of a planned housing development site has shown that the site has a history\nof mining activity in the local area. The site has ground with good bearing capacity.\nExplain which type of foundation should be used on this project.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for a correct identification of\nthe foundation type and one additional mark for\nappropriate justification of that selection up to a\nmaximum of two marks.\n• A raft foundation is the appropriate foundation\nfor this project (1) because a raft will move\nwith the ground (during periods of\nsubsidence) (1)\n• A raft foundation is the appropriate foundation\nfor this project (1) because a raft will rigidly\nsupport the structure during ground\nmovement (1)\nDo not accept generic responses\nabout other potential benefits of\nraft foundations not specific to\nlocations with a history of mining\nactivity in the area.\nMarking points may be reversed/given in any\norder.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 14, "sub": "", "marks": 2, "section": "B", "ctx": [], "text": "Explain how interstitial condensation can be avoided within timber frame\nconstruction with a plasterboard and skim inner surface.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for a method and one\nadditional mark for appropriate justification of\nthat method up to a maximum of two marks.\n• A vapour control layer can be added behind\nthe plasterboard (1) because it will prevent\nwater vapour passing through the insulation\nand condensing (1)\n• Foil backed/vapour check plasterboard could\nbe specified (1) because this will prevent\nwater vapour passing through the insulation\nand condensing without an additional\nmembrane (1)\nMarking points may be reversed/given in any\norder.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 15, "sub": "", "marks": 2, "section": "B", "ctx": [], "text": "A housing development is being constructed on a site where large mature trees\nare present. Ground investigations indicate that the subsoil is a firm clay to a depth\nof 3.5m.\nExplain one reason why a 750mm deep strip foundation would not be used on this\nproject.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for a correct reason and one\nadditional mark for appropriate justification of\nthat risk up to a maximum of two marks.\n• In the presence of clay foundations need to be\na minimum of 900mm deep (1) because clays\nare susceptible to shrinkage and\nheave/swelling (1)\n• When trees are present the foundation depth\nneed to be much deeper/is calculated using\nthe ratios of tree height and distance (1)\nbecause trees extract water from the ground\ncausing excessive ground shrinkage during\ndry weather which has less impact at greater\ndepths (1)\nMarking points may be reversed/given in any\norder.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 16, "sub": "", "marks": 9, "section": "B", "ctx": [], "text": "A new school is to be built on the site of an old demolished factory building.\nThe factory building had cellars that were used for the storage of industrial chemicals.\nThe factory offices were heated via a gas central heating system and the production\nareas contained a number of gas fired furnaces and kilns.\nThe school is to be constructed using deep foundations.\nGround water was found to be present at a depth of 1.5m.\nDiscuss the safe working practices that will need to be considered when operatives\nare working in the deep excavations.", "ms": {"body": "Indicative content:\n• Reference to specific hazards and risks\n• Recognition that working in deep excavations\nand confined spaces both apply to this\nscenario\n• Consideration of ground stability and methods\nof earthwork support\n• Overburden risks, considerations and\nmitigations\n• Safe access and egress\n• Water in excavations and drowning risks\n• Associated pumping and removal of water\n• Issues of air quality and contamination\nincluding hazards and risks from disused gas\nservices\n• Possible oxygen depletion\n• Investigation into the presence of industrial\nchemicals\n• Consideration of the potential need for of\nrespirators/breathing apparatus\n• Use of buddy system and communication\nmethods/equipment\n• Potential fire or explosion risk from potential\ngas leaks/trapped pockets of gas\n• Air quality monitoring\n• Available fire precautions, e.g. extinguishers\netc.\n• Length of exposure\n• Consideration of items falling into trench from\nabove\n• Use of general PPE in addition to task specific\nPPE\n9 39\nLevel\nDescriptors\nThe second trait (application) carries twice as much weighting as trait 1\n(analysis).\n0\nNo rewardable material\n1\n1 - 3\n• Demonstrates a basic analysis of the\nsituation by superficially breaking down the\ndifferent aspects into their component\nparts.\n• Demonstrates basic application of\nknowledge and understanding that is\npartially relevant to the context of the\nquestion and may consider only one side of\nthe context.\n2\n4 - 6\n• Demonstrates a good analysis of the\nsituation by breaking down the different\naspects into their component parts.\n• Demonstrates good application of\nknowledge and understanding that is\nrelevant to the context of the question and\nconsiders both sides of the context.\n7 - 9\n• Demonstrates a thorough analysis of the\nsituation by comprehensively breaking\ndown the different aspects into their\ncomponent parts.\n• Demonstrates comprehensive application of\nknowledge and understanding that is\nconsistently relevant to the context of the\nquestion and considers both sides of the\ncontext in a balanced way.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 17, "sub": "", "marks": 9, "section": "B", "ctx": [], "text": "When designing new housing the architect is deciding whether to use trussed rafter\nroof construction or traditional timber roof construction comprising rafters and\npurlins with separate ceiling joists.\nThe architect is designing to a tight budget so will need to utilise the solution that\ngives the lowest cost.\nDiscuss the technical factors the architect would need to consider when deciding to\nuse trussed rafter roofing or traditional timber roofing for the construction which will\nhave gable end roofs.\nIn your answer you should consider both:\n• The materials being used\n• The methods of construction.", "ms": {"body": "Trussed rafter roofing:\n• A mix of on-site work and prefabrication\n• A rapid on-site method of roof construction\n• A cost effective form of construction when\nworking to tight budgets\n• Prefabrication of the trussed rafters has\nsustainability benefits such as reduced site\nwaste\n• Trussed rafters are computer designed for the\nspecific loads and spans of the building\n• Still requires some on site carpentry work such\nas binders and wind bracing to prevent\ndistortion and racking\n• Requires vertical and lateral restraint\n• If the building is timber framed the roof can be\nerected on the substructure at ground level\nand craned into position on the day of timber\nframe erection\n• Any water tanks in the roof space need to be\nconsidered in terms of loading at the time of\ndesign\n• The ties and struts within the trussed rafters\nimpact on the usability of the loft space\n• Interior roof space can however still be\nincorporated via the use of special attic trusses\n• Still perceived as a modern method despite\nhaving been in use for many years (introduced\nin 1964)\nTraditional timber roofing:\n• Requires the installation of separate rafters,\npurlins, ridge boards, ceiling joists etc.\n• The timber used will have to be stress graded\ntimber which is expensive\n• Purlins will need loadbearing wall or truss\nsupport\n• Collars may need to be used to prevent rafter\nspread and associated lateral forces on the\nsupporting walls\n• More highly skilled workers required and\nerection is more time consuming\n• The ends of all rafters need to be cut to the\ncorrect angle and may need to be ‘birdsmouth’\nnotched over the wallplate\n• More expensive and costly method of\nconstruction however can be cost effective if\nthe roofspace is used as this increases the floor\narea of the houses without building an\nadditional storey.\n9 41\n• The higher cost can however be offset by gains\nin terms of interior space\n• More on-site waste produced\n• Does not need to be computer designed as\n‘deemed to satisfy’ provisions are available in\ntabulated form in the building regulations\n• Each individual component needs to be sized to\nensure they are structurally appropriate\n• Provides an interior space that is can be used\nas a room provided that the joists are sized to\nfloor joist requirements rather than ceiling joist\nrequirements\n• Dormer windows and velum rooflights can\neasily be incorporated via appropriately\ntrimmed openings\n• Public perception is that traditional timber roof\na higher quality more durable form of\nconstruction\n• Larger bedrooms will be possibly if the roof\nspace is used as a bedroom\nLevel\nDescriptors\nThe second trait (application) carries twice as much weighting as trait 1\n(analysis).\n0\nNo rewardable material\n1\n1 - 3\n• Demonstrates a basic analysis of the\nsituation by superficially breaking down\nthe different aspects into their component\nparts.\n• Demonstrates basic application of\nknowledge and understanding that is\npartially relevant to the context of the\nquestion and may consider only one side of\nthe context.\n2\n4 - 6\n• Demonstrates a good analysis of the\nsituation by breaking down the different\naspects into their component parts.\n• Demonstrates good application of\nknowledge and understanding that is\nrelevant to the context of the question and\nconsiders both sides of the context.\n7 - 9\n• Demonstrates a thorough analysis of the\nsituation by comprehensively breaking\ndown the different aspects into their\ncomponent parts.\n• Demonstrates comprehensive application\nof knowledge and understanding that is\nconsistently relevant to the context of the\nquestion and considers both sides of the\ncontext in a balanced way.\n42\nApril 2026\nFor information about Pearson Qualifications, including Pearson Edexcel\nand BTEC qualifications visit qualifications.pearson.com\nEdexcel and BTEC are registered trademarks of Pearson Education Limited\nPearson Education Limited. Registered in England and Wales No. 872828\nRegistered Office: 80 Strand, London WC2R 0RL.\nVAT Reg No GB 278 537121", "levels": null}}], "badge": "Pearson · current spec", "badgeTone": "new", "notice": "Pearson’s sample assessment for the current AAQ specification: the closest match to the real exam. Figures, tables and information booklets are described in words."}]);
